230
00:19:37,884 --> 00:19:39,803
Large coffee.

231
00:19:46,351 --> 00:19:47,936
You have had many, senor.

232
00:19:56,736 --> 00:19:59,280
Yeah! Yeah! Yeah!

233
00:20:06,663 --> 00:20:08,498
Get out of here!

234
00:20:10,125 --> 00:20:11,376
Come back. Hey.

235
00:20:14,087 --> 00:20:15,130
Come here.

236
00:21:10,852 --> 00:21:12,395
Welcome, Jim.

237
00:21:13,855 --> 00:21:15,207
Please turn your attention

238
00:21:15,231 --> 00:21:18,544
to the screens displaying safety tips.

239
00:21:18,568 --> 00:21:22,882
These spacesuits are designed
to withstand the harsh environment of space.

240
00:21:22,906 --> 00:21:25,176
The carbon fiber and polyamide construction

241
00:21:25,200 --> 00:21:28,578
means your suit is both flexible and durable.

242
00:21:41,007 --> 00:21:43,718
Remember, your spacesuit is your lifeline.

243
00:21:52,936 --> 00:21:56,731
Slide the handle on the right
to release the air pressure.

244
00:21:58,066 --> 00:22:00,419
Your magnetic boots are now engaged.

245
00:22:00,443 --> 00:22:03,756
They can be deactivated
using the control panel on your arm.

246
00:22:03,780 --> 00:22:06,324
Press the red button
to open the airlock door.

247
00:22:10,328 --> 00:22:12,080
Have a wonderful time.

248
00:22:19,587 --> 00:22:20,630
Tether attached.

249
00:23:49,177 --> 00:23:50,678
Welcome back, Jim.

250
00:23:54,307 --> 00:23:56,535
We hope you decide to join us again soon

251
00:23:56,559 --> 00:23:58,937
for another thrilling experience.

252
00:24:22,919 --> 00:24:24,879
Have a wonderful time.

253
00:25:45,460 --> 00:25:47,045
Aurora.

254
00:25:48,755 --> 00:25:50,840
Searching passenger profiles.

255
00:25:51,132 --> 00:25:52,943
I'm Aurora Lane.

256
00:25:52,967 --> 00:25:55,928
Passenger 1456. I'm a writer.

257
00:25:56,220 --> 00:25:57,781
I think we tell each other stories

258
00:25:57,805 --> 00:26:00,475
to know we're not alone, to make contact.

259
00:26:00,808 --> 00:26:04,121
Your father was Oliver Lane,
a pulitzer prize-winning author.

260
00:26:04,145 --> 00:26:05,497
No pressure, right?

261
00:26:05,521 --> 00:26:08,959
My dad used to say,
"if you live an ordinary life,

262
00:26:08,983 --> 00:26:11,336
“all you'll have are ordinary stories.

263
00:26:11,360 --> 00:26:13,529
"You have to live a life of adventure."

264
00:26:14,864 --> 00:26:17,700
So... Here I am.

265
00:26:24,749 --> 00:26:26,334
Good morning.

266
00:26:28,836 --> 00:26:31,798
We're starting over in every way.

267
00:26:32,465 --> 00:26:35,110
I'll have to figure out
where to live, how to live,

268
00:26:35,134 --> 00:26:36,695
who my friends will be.

269
00:26:36,719 --> 00:26:38,072
It's like the first day of school.

270
00:26:38,096 --> 00:26:40,223
If the school bus took 120 years to get there.

271
00:26:43,434 --> 00:26:46,538
We're creating a culture...
You are funny.

272
00:26:46,562 --> 00:26:49,273
Do you ever read something
and feel like it's written just for you?

273
00:26:50,024 --> 00:26:51,859
I don't do a lot of reading.

274
00:26:52,110 --> 00:26:53,736
She's good.

275
00:26:54,654 --> 00:26:56,006
Who's that?

276
00:26:56,030 --> 00:26:57,448
Aurora.

277
00:26:58,950 --> 00:27:00,076
The sleeping girl.

278
00:27:05,164 --> 00:27:07,267
You know, I'm not saying the universe is evil,

279
00:27:07,291 --> 00:27:09,144
but it sure has a nasty sense of humor.

280
00:27:09,168 --> 00:27:10,229
How is that?

281
00:27:10,253 --> 00:27:12,815
You get to fly to another planet,

282
00:27:12,839 --> 00:27:15,299
but you'll die along the way.

283
00:27:16,384 --> 00:27:18,886
And you find the perfect woman

284
00:27:20,221 --> 00:27:21,973
right in front of you...

285
00:27:23,141 --> 00:27:24,934
Yet she's completely out of reach.

286
00:27:28,563 --> 00:27:30,231
Yeah, I'll miss New York City.

287
00:27:30,815 --> 00:27:32,918
Give me a cup of coffee
and a view of the Chrysler building,

288
00:27:32,942 --> 00:27:34,193
and I can write all day.

289
00:27:34,652 --> 00:27:36,755
They do have coffee on homestead ll, right?

290
00:27:36,779 --> 00:27:37,881
They better have coffee.

291
00:27:37,905 --> 00:27:40,283
If they don't, I have to turn around
and come back to earth.

292
00:27:41,492 --> 00:27:43,536
Is there anything you regret leaving behind?

293
00:27:44,537 --> 00:27:48,082
Yeah, I'll miss the trees in central park
turning in the fall.

294
00:27:49,584 --> 00:27:51,127
I'll also miss crowds...

295
00:28:00,845 --> 00:28:02,597
Say you were trapped on a desert island,

296
00:28:03,264 --> 00:28:05,993
and you had the power
to wish somebody there with you.

297
00:28:06,017 --> 00:28:07,810
You wouldn't be alone anymore.

298
00:28:08,769 --> 00:28:10,998
But you'd be stranding the person
on the island.

299
00:28:11,022 --> 00:28:13,500
Would you make that wish?

300
00:28:13,524 --> 00:28:15,919
I don't know. I've never been on an island.

301
00:28:15,943 --> 00:28:17,612
Okay, well, yeah.

302
00:28:18,571 --> 00:28:19,614
Forget the island.

303
00:28:21,115 --> 00:28:25,119
Let's say you figured out
how to do something

304
00:28:25,786 --> 00:28:27,514
that would make your life
a million times better,

305
00:28:27,538 --> 00:28:30,184
but you knew it was wrong
and there's no taking it back.

306
00:28:30,208 --> 00:28:31,292
How do you do the math?

307
00:28:31,667 --> 00:28:33,753
Jim, these are not robot questions.

308
00:28:40,218 --> 00:28:41,719
I know how to wake Aurora up.

309
00:28:42,762 --> 00:28:44,573
Well, that seems like a fine idea.

310
00:28:44,597 --> 00:28:45,681
You could use some company.

311
00:28:45,973 --> 00:28:48,476
I'd be stranding her on this ship
for the rest of her life.

312
00:28:49,477 --> 00:28:50,770
Well, you can't do that.

313
00:28:52,313 --> 00:28:53,522
What am I gonna do?

314
00:28:53,648 --> 00:28:55,125
I'm here for you.

315
00:28:55,149 --> 00:28:56,943
Arthur, you're a machine.

316
00:29:01,322 --> 00:29:04,325
See? You can't feel that.
You don't have feelings.

317
00:29:07,203 --> 00:29:08,430
See?

318
00:29:08,454 --> 00:29:10,182
That doesn't hurt.

319
00:29:10,206 --> 00:29:11,624
And you don't even mind.

320
00:29:12,625 --> 00:29:15,336
'Cause you're not a person.

321
00:29:22,051 --> 00:29:23,487
Don't even think about it.

322
00:29:23,511 --> 00:29:24,988
No more Aurora talk.

323
00:29:25,012 --> 00:29:26,430
I'm over it.

324
00:29:26,973 --> 00:29:27,974
I'm moving on.

325
00:29:29,308 --> 00:29:31,161
Don't even think about it!

326
00:29:31,185 --> 00:29:33,121
What am I gonna do?
I'm just gonna sit and think about it?

327
00:29:33,145 --> 00:29:34,706
I can't think about it, so I'm not going to.

328
00:29:34,730 --> 00:29:36,625
That's the deal. I've made my decision.

329
00:29:36,649 --> 00:29:37,650
I know what I'm doing.

330
00:29:38,401 --> 00:29:39,443
I just cut it off.

331
00:29:42,029 --> 00:29:43,090
I'm not... no more.

332
00:29:43,114 --> 00:29:44,716
You won't hear me bring up her name again.

333
00:29:44,740 --> 00:29:46,033
I'll never even mention her name again.

334
00:29:46,492 --> 00:29:47,493
Done.

335
00:29:50,538 --> 00:29:52,206
You can't do it.

336
00:29:57,378 --> 00:29:58,796
Shaving off my beard.

337
00:30:09,724 --> 00:30:11,559
Please don't do it.

338
00:32:04,004 --> 00:32:05,482
Good morning, Aurora.

339
00:32:05,506 --> 00:32:07,734
How are you feeling?

340
00:32:07,758 --> 00:32:10,553
It's perfectly normal to feel confused.

341
00:32:10,678 --> 00:32:13,764
You just spent 120 years
in suspended animation.

342
00:33:02,146 --> 00:33:04,249
Grand concourse.

343
00:33:04,273 --> 00:33:05,733
Hello?

344
00:33:09,236 --> 00:33:10,488
Anybody?

345
00:33:19,371 --> 00:33:20,372
Hello.

346
00:33:24,251 --> 00:33:25,252
Hi.

347
00:33:28,589 --> 00:33:30,382
Are you passenger or crew?

348
00:33:32,593 --> 00:33:34,321
Passenger.

349
00:33:34,345 --> 00:33:35,572
Jim Preston.

350
00:33:35,596 --> 00:33:37,223
Aurora Lane.

351
00:33:38,641 --> 00:33:40,494
Do you know what's going on?

352
00:33:40,518 --> 00:33:42,436
Nobody else from my row woke up.

353
00:33:43,771 --> 00:33:44,915
Same for me.

354
00:33:44,939 --> 00:33:47,709
The crew's supposed to wake up
a month before we do,

355
00:33:47,733 --> 00:33:49,485
but I haven't seen anybody.

356
00:33:53,405 --> 00:33:54,865
The crew is still asleep.

357
00:33:56,951 --> 00:33:58,661
Are you saying nobody's awake?

358
00:33:59,411 --> 00:34:00,454
Just me.

359
00:34:00,871 --> 00:34:01,956
Just you?

360
00:34:02,122 --> 00:34:03,415
It's just us.

361
00:34:04,416 --> 00:34:06,752
But somebody's got to land the ship
in a few weeks.

362
00:34:07,586 --> 00:34:10,881
We will arrive in approximately 89 years.

363
00:34:11,131 --> 00:34:13,133
Eighty-nine years?

364
00:34:15,427 --> 00:34:18,264
The other passengers aren't late waking up.

365
00:34:19,848 --> 00:34:21,433
We were early.

366
00:34:26,021 --> 00:34:28,375
We need help.

367
00:34:28,399 --> 00:34:29,650
Where's the crew?

368
00:34:30,276 --> 00:34:33,004
The crew's in a secure hibernation room.

369
00:34:33,028 --> 00:34:36,383
Everything important, the controls,
the reactors, the engines...

370
00:34:36,407 --> 00:34:38,051
It's all behind firewalls.

371
00:34:38,075 --> 00:34:39,743
There's no way through.

372
00:34:50,212 --> 00:34:52,464
How long have you been awake?

373
00:34:53,173 --> 00:34:54,883
A year and three weeks.

374
00:34:55,801 --> 00:34:58,345
No. No, no, no.

375
00:34:59,179 --> 00:35:00,639
No, this can't be happening.

376
00:35:01,390 --> 00:35:03,183
We have to go back to sleep.

377
00:35:03,892 --> 00:35:05,185
Aurora, we can't.

378
00:35:07,980 --> 00:35:11,358
We just have to get back to our pods
and start them up again.

379
00:35:12,651 --> 00:35:15,338
I can't find my pod. I can't find my pod.

380
00:35:15,362 --> 00:35:17,674
I can't find my pod. I don't know which...

381
00:35:17,698 --> 00:35:19,158
It doesn't matter.

382
00:35:20,242 --> 00:35:21,845
- Aurora...
- I can't find it!

383
00:35:21,869 --> 00:35:23,680
- Stop. Aurora...
- I can't find which one is mine!

384
00:35:23,704 --> 00:35:25,432
- Stop.
- I don't know which one is mine!

385
00:35:25,456 --> 00:35:26,558
- I'll help you.
- I can't...

386
00:35:26,582 --> 00:35:29,126
Stop! It doesn't matter.

387
00:35:31,754 --> 00:35:35,090
Putting somebody into hibernation
requires special equipment.

388
00:35:35,716 --> 00:35:37,694
Remember the facility
where they put us under,

389
00:35:37,718 --> 00:35:40,095
all the procedures we went through?

390
00:35:40,220 --> 00:35:42,490
These pods are designed
to keep us in hibernation,

391
00:35:42,514 --> 00:35:44,117
to wake us up at the right time,

392
00:35:44,141 --> 00:35:45,768
but they can't put us back to sleep.

393
00:35:45,976 --> 00:35:48,729
You don't think there's a way
back into hibernation?

394
00:35:50,230 --> 00:35:51,666
No.

395
00:35:51,690 --> 00:35:53,233
But there has to be.

396
00:35:54,068 --> 00:35:55,527
There has to be.

397
00:36:04,411 --> 00:36:07,164
9:00. Nighttime.

398
00:36:12,086 --> 00:36:15,089
I know I should be
working the problem, but...

399
00:36:15,756 --> 00:36:17,508
I can't even keep my eyes open.

400
00:36:18,634 --> 00:36:20,528
You just came out of hibernation.

401
00:36:20,552 --> 00:36:23,073
It's going to be a couple of days
before you're 100%.

402
00:36:23,097 --> 00:36:24,515
You should get some rest.

403
00:36:25,724 --> 00:36:27,309
Think I'm gonna have to.

404
00:36:28,686 --> 00:36:30,038
I'll walk you to your cabin.

405
00:36:30,062 --> 00:36:32,606
No, it's okay. I'll be all right.

406
00:36:33,482 --> 00:36:34,483
Okay.

407
00:36:38,362 --> 00:36:39,405
Good night, Aurora.

408
00:36:41,949 --> 00:36:43,301
More than a year?

409
00:36:43,325 --> 00:36:45,119
I can't imagine.

410
00:36:45,911 --> 00:36:47,371
It must have been so hard for you.

411
00:36:48,539 --> 00:36:49,974
It was.

412
00:36:49,998 --> 00:36:51,458
Good night, Jim.

413
00:36:53,293 --> 00:36:56,338
Please buckle up and secure any loose items.

414
00:36:59,717 --> 00:37:01,969
- Whiskey, neat.
- Sure thing.

415
00:37:08,016 --> 00:37:09,101
How's your day been?

416
00:37:10,978 --> 00:37:12,104
Aurora's awake.

417
00:37:12,396 --> 00:37:13,981
Congratulations.

418
00:37:16,233 --> 00:37:17,776
You don't look happy.

419
00:37:21,947 --> 00:37:23,675
Arthur, can you keep a secret?

420
00:37:23,699 --> 00:37:25,260
Jim...

421
00:37:25,284 --> 00:37:27,828
I'm not just a bartender, I'm a gentleman.

422
00:37:28,495 --> 00:37:30,789
Don't tell Aurora that I woke her up.

423
00:37:33,125 --> 00:37:35,586
She thinks it was an accident. Let me tell her.

424
00:37:35,836 --> 00:37:37,254
Of course.

425
00:37:50,851 --> 00:37:53,163
How can there be no way
to put someone back into hibernation?

426
00:37:53,187 --> 00:37:54,664
What if a pod breaks down?

427
00:37:54,688 --> 00:37:58,817
No hibernation pod has malfunctioned
in thousands of interstellar flights.

428
00:37:59,485 --> 00:38:02,088
Well, I'm awake.

429
00:38:02,112 --> 00:38:04,406
Hibernation pods are fail-safe.

430
00:38:05,032 --> 00:38:06,342
Good morning.

431
00:38:06,366 --> 00:38:09,345
- Have you eaten?
- No. I'm starving.

432
00:38:09,369 --> 00:38:11,663
And this is the dumbest machine.

433
00:38:11,789 --> 00:38:13,332
Happy to help.

434
00:38:26,094 --> 00:38:28,055
Gold-class breakfast.

435
00:38:38,607 --> 00:38:40,192
Well, you're a man of simple tastes.

436
00:38:40,859 --> 00:38:42,945
I'm not a gold-class passenger.

437
00:38:45,280 --> 00:38:47,467
French breakfast puff's above my pay grade.

438
00:38:47,491 --> 00:38:49,010
What? This whole time?

439
00:38:49,034 --> 00:38:50,845
- Yeah.
- What can I get you?

440
00:38:50,869 --> 00:38:52,889
- No. No, I'm fine.
- Stop it.

441
00:38:52,913 --> 00:38:54,706
Really. Okay.

442
00:38:56,458 --> 00:38:57,751
Here you go.

443
00:39:02,673 --> 00:39:03,674
Yeah.

444
00:39:04,299 --> 00:39:07,529
So, I was thinking,
maybe there's another way to go to sleep.

445
00:39:07,553 --> 00:39:08,822
What about the infirmary?

446
00:39:08,846 --> 00:39:11,282
I checked it out.
It's just scanners and an autodoc.

447
00:39:11,306 --> 00:39:13,827
There could be another hibernation machine
in the cargo hold.

448
00:39:13,851 --> 00:39:14,977
I had that thought, too.

449
00:39:15,227 --> 00:39:16,371
And then I read the manifest.

450
00:39:16,395 --> 00:39:18,438
It's mostly farming stuff,
machines and trade goods.

451
00:39:18,939 --> 00:39:21,209
Replacement parts
for computers and engineering.

452
00:39:21,233 --> 00:39:23,670
We're not gonna find
a hibernation facility in a box.

453
00:39:23,694 --> 00:39:25,463
- We could build our own.
- We can't.

454
00:39:25,487 --> 00:39:26,631
Jim, you're not even trying.

455
00:39:26,655 --> 00:39:28,949
I have tried everything.

456
00:39:29,283 --> 00:39:31,034
For over a year, I...

457
00:39:32,286 --> 00:39:33,412
I tried everything.

458
00:39:33,787 --> 00:39:34,913
Well...

459
00:39:36,540 --> 00:39:38,083
Well, I'm not ready to give up.

460
00:39:47,467 --> 00:39:49,219
Infirmary.

461
00:39:57,019 --> 00:39:59,873
What about research articles?
Any kind of technical documents.

462
00:39:59,897 --> 00:40:02,542
Hibernation technology is proprietary.

463
00:40:02,566 --> 00:40:06,278
The following articles deal with the subject
on a theoretical level.

464
00:40:49,529 --> 00:40:51,865
New file. My voyage.

465
00:40:53,033 --> 00:40:56,703
I boarded the avalon with an idea,
a destination.

466
00:40:57,537 --> 00:41:00,874
Both now out of reach.

467
00:41:03,335 --> 00:41:04,878
I've been awake for seven days.

468
00:41:05,837 --> 00:41:07,965
Awake far too soon.

469
00:41:08,298 --> 00:41:10,550
And I may well spend the rest of my life here,

470
00:41:11,343 --> 00:41:15,138
in a steel world 1,000 meters long.

471
00:41:16,556 --> 00:41:20,936
There's another passenger awake,
a mechanic named Jim Preston.

472
00:41:23,063 --> 00:41:25,732
He seems to have accepted our fate.

473
00:41:26,566 --> 00:41:28,402
But I'm scared.

474
00:41:29,361 --> 00:41:30,988
I'm fighting to stay calm.

475
00:41:33,907 --> 00:41:36,302
All the other passengers will sleep

476
00:41:36,326 --> 00:41:38,328
for another 90 years

477
00:41:39,705 --> 00:41:42,165
while I live out my life on this ship,

478
00:41:42,916 --> 00:41:44,626
traveling forever...

479
00:41:45,627 --> 00:41:47,462
Never arriving...

480
00:41:48,422 --> 00:41:50,799
My only companion a total stranger.

481
00:41:59,975 --> 00:42:01,101
Why did you do it?

482
00:42:03,770 --> 00:42:04,855
Do what?

483
00:42:06,481 --> 00:42:08,942
Emigrate. Leave earth.

484
00:42:09,943 --> 00:42:11,445
I'm interviewing you.

485
00:42:11,611 --> 00:42:12,612
You're what?

486
00:42:12,821 --> 00:42:16,342
You were the first hibernation failure
in the history of space travel.

487
00:42:16,366 --> 00:42:17,451
That makes you a story.

488
00:42:18,535 --> 00:42:19,887
Who you gonna tell?

489
00:42:19,911 --> 00:42:21,139
Posterity.

490
00:42:21,163 --> 00:42:23,957
So, why did you give up your life on earth?

491
00:42:24,499 --> 00:42:28,378
120-year space hibernation means
you'll never see your family or friends again.

492
00:42:28,879 --> 00:42:32,817
You'll wake up in a new century
on a new planet.

493
00:42:32,841 --> 00:42:34,551
It's the ultimate geographical suicide.

494
00:42:35,135 --> 00:42:37,447
Well, I could ask you the same thing.

495
00:42:37,471 --> 00:42:39,306
But it's my interview.

496
00:42:39,681 --> 00:42:41,600
Were you running away from something?

497
00:42:41,767 --> 00:42:43,369
No.

498
00:42:43,393 --> 00:42:45,788
- Everything was okay.
- So?

499
00:42:45,812 --> 00:42:47,290
Well, I guess I just wanted a new world,

500
00:42:47,314 --> 00:42:48,482
I don't know, a fresh start.

501
00:42:48,648 --> 00:42:50,626
That's homestead company advertising.

502
00:42:50,650 --> 00:42:52,027
- Is it?
- Jim.

503
00:42:52,194 --> 00:42:53,987
I know. I guess. You're right.

504
00:42:59,534 --> 00:43:02,329
Back on earth, when something breaks,
you don't fix it, you replace it.

505
00:43:03,205 --> 00:43:05,916
The colonies, they have problems to solve.

506
00:43:06,166 --> 00:43:07,834
They're my kind of problems.

507
00:43:09,544 --> 00:43:11,689
And a mechanic is somebody.

508
00:43:11,713 --> 00:43:13,924
This is a new world still being built.

509
00:43:16,093 --> 00:43:18,303
I could build a house and live in it.

510
00:43:23,183 --> 00:43:25,852
Open country. Room to grow.

511
00:43:26,144 --> 00:43:27,395
Now you're back to slogans.

512
00:43:27,813 --> 00:43:29,231
Can't slogans be true?

513
00:43:31,149 --> 00:43:32,543
Do you know how much
homestead company

514
00:43:32,567 --> 00:43:34,712
made off its first planet?

515
00:43:34,736 --> 00:43:36,839
Eight quadrillion dollars.

516
00:43:36,863 --> 00:43:39,675
That's eight million billions.

517
00:43:39,699 --> 00:43:42,077
Colony planets
are the biggest business going.

518
00:43:42,536 --> 00:43:44,204
Did you pay full price for your ticket?

519
00:43:44,371 --> 00:43:46,682
No. I'm in a desirable trade.

520
00:43:46,706 --> 00:43:51,354
So they fill your head with dreams,
discount your ticket,

521
00:43:51,378 --> 00:43:53,314
and you fly off to populate their planet

522
00:43:53,338 --> 00:43:55,483
and give homestead
20% of everything that you make

523
00:43:55,507 --> 00:43:56,567
for the rest of your life.

524
00:43:56,591 --> 00:43:59,779
Not to mention the debt you run up
on this fancy starship.

525
00:43:59,803 --> 00:44:01,888
So all you see here is 5,000 suckers?

526
00:44:03,014 --> 00:44:06,393
I see zeros
on the homestead company's bottom line.

527
00:44:06,810 --> 00:44:09,771
I see 5,000 men and women
changing their lives.

528
00:44:10,689 --> 00:44:13,358
For 5,000 different reasons.
You don't know these people.

529
00:44:13,483 --> 00:44:16,111
I'm a journalist. I know people.

530
00:44:17,779 --> 00:44:18,780
Really?

531
00:44:19,573 --> 00:44:20,574
This one.

532
00:44:21,032 --> 00:44:23,535
Is he a banker, a teacher or a gardener?

533
00:44:25,162 --> 00:44:26,913
- Banker.
- He's a gardener.

534
